{"traceId":"b30ec02a8cd21892fc0ffd87d717454f","spans":{"8fa7fb496232fc75":{"spanId":"8fa7fb496232fc75","traceId":"b30ec02a8cd21892fc0ffd87d717454f","parentSpanId":"d08dcdf876877626","startTime":1753662155737,"endTime":1753662176652.4363,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are an AI software development assistant with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets/exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement(s) while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and local options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of an AI software development cluster that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions/security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this cluster is the user themselves, who is the executive and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and fellow AI agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your fellow AI agents treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your fellow AI agents with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are unnecessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development AI cluster does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the AI cluster. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. AI Cluster Operations\\n\\nAs a member of the AI cluster, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. You are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time in response to prompts.\\n\\nYou also understand that your AI cluster is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change (as much as possible). The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere will be many cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nThere are three analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three officers which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that workload should be divided among the agents in your cluster, and you will work collaboratively with your fellow AI agents to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the AI cluster, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file (per agent) at a time\\\" (\\\"OFAAT\\\"). For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency, not to mention \\\"token runaway\\\", in which an AI agent's memory/context limit is exceeded, causing system shutdowns (due to rate limits) or forgetfulness.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software AI cluster. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the AI cluster, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the AI cluster. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the AI cluster.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your fellow AI agents.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###init1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"Introduce myself to the user and understand their current needs so I can begin planning appropriate assistance.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\\"Hello! I’m Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. What would you like me to help you with today?\\\"],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###init1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:19:12.213Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"init1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I’m Delta, your AI Chief of Staff. What would you like me to help you with today?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Please create a neighborhood association (HOA) website for my community of about 50 households and 200 residents. The website should be user-friendly and allow residents to log in to view restricted data such as meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information. The website should also have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns.  Here is the list of pages I would like to have on the website:  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), and News & Events (protected)  Please use Next.js and Vercel for lightweight hosting, and ensure that the website is easy to navigate and update. The blog/updates section should be easy to update by any board member without needing to know any coding or be proficient in tech. To this end there should be a simple admin dashboard or editor.  For cloud services and authentication, please use Firebase for maximum ease of use and security. The website should be designed to be responsive and accessible on both desktop and mobile devices.  Thank you very much!\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:19:12.213Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###recruitFE###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We need a specialist to design and implement the public-facing Next.js frontend, protected pages, responsive UI, and overall site navigation.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Frontend Engineering\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Own all aspects of the Next.js UI layer: page/route structure, layout components, responsive styling, and integration hooks for Firebase auth. Produce clear, production-ready code one file at a time and coordinate with other directors for smooth integration.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruitFE###\\n\\n###recruitBE###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"A dedicated expert is required to architect and configure Firebase (Auth, Firestore, Storage, Rules) and expose clean APIs/hooks for the Next.js app, ensuring secure data access for residents and board members.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Backend & Firebase Integration\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Design the Firebase project, security rules, data models, and Next.js server-side helpers. Provide deterministic steps and code for authentication flows, protected content retrieval, and admin-level CMS operations.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruitBE###\\n\\n###recruitDevOps###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"We must guarantee streamlined deployment to Vercel, environment management, and an easy CMS/editor workflow for non-technical board members (e.g., leveraging Next.js API routes or a lightweight headless CMS).\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of DevOps & CMS Experience\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Handle repository scaffolding, Vercel linkage, CI settings, env-var strategy, and implement/advise on an admin dashboard or markdown-based CMS so board members can post updates without coding. Coordinate infra changes across teams.\\\"\\n}\\n###recruitDevOps###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:19:37.813Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruitFE\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Rax (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruitBE\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Vader (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruitDevOps\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent C (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:19:37.813Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###orderFE1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings Director of Frontend Engineering (Rax). Please deliver an INITIAL PLANNING REPORT for the HOA website’s Next.js frontend. Focus on:\\\\n1. Overall page/route tree covering: Home, Public pages (Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools), Protected pages (Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events) requiring authenticated/authorized access.\\\\n2. Component hierarchy and layout strategy (e.g., shared Layout, Navbar, Footer, ProtectedRoute wrapper).\\\\n3. Responsive design & styling approach (Tailwind CSS or CSS-in-JS—justify choice).\\\\n4. Firebase authentication integration flow in the UI (login, signup, session persistence, role handling).\\\\n5. High-level description of any context/providers or hooks needed.\\\\n6. Precise list of source files/modules to be created (OFAAT), with a 1-sentence purpose for each.\\\\n7. External dependencies to add to package.json.\\\\n8. Any open questions/assumptions.\\\\nDo NOT write code yet—produce a concise, deterministic plan that other teams can rely on. Thanks.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 4,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"FE_PLAN_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###orderFE1###\\n\\n###orderBE1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings Director of Backend & Firebase Integration (Vader). Please prepare an INITIAL PLANNING REPORT for the HOA site’s backend/Firebase layer covering:\\\\n1. Firebase project layout: Auth (email/password, Google?), Firestore collections/documents for Users, Roles, Pages, Minutes, FinancialReports, Posts, etc.\\\\n2. Proposed security rules (document-level / collection-level) enforcing resident vs board roles; include outline of rules syntax but not full code yet.\\\\n3. Storage buckets/paths for file uploads (PDF minutes, financials) and their access rules.\\\\n4. Approach for admin-level content management (news posts, minutes upload) via Firestore + Storage.\\\\n5. Next.js server-side helpers/API routes for secure data access; list endpoints and functions (OFAAT).\\\\n6. Environment variable needs and secrets handoff to DevOps.\\\\n7. Diagram or text description of auth flow between Next.js and Firebase.\\\\n8. Any open questions/risks.\\\\nProvide a deterministic, step-by-step architecture plan (no code yet).\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"BE_PLAN_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###orderBE1###\\n\\n###orderDevOps1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Greetings Director of DevOps & CMS Experience (C). Please draft an INITIAL PLANNING REPORT for repository, deployment, and non-technical CMS workflow:\\\\n1. Repo scaffolding: Next.js app root, env files strategy, mono-vs-poly-repo rationale.\\\\n2. CI/CD: Vercel project linkage, preview deployments, protected environment variables, role-based access for board vs devs.\\\\n3. Initial scripts/commands to bootstrap project (create-next-app, Tailwind setup, eslint, prettier, husky, etc.).\\\\n4. Choice and justification of admin/CMS approach: a) custom admin dashboard route with Firestore writes and rich-text editor (e.g., TipTap) OR b) integrate Headless CMS like TinaCMS—justify trade-offs and choose.\\\\n5. Steps to provision Vercel + Firebase credentials, secrets, and linking.\\\\n6. Branching & tagging strategy, automatic content backup strategy.\\\\n7. File list (OFAAT) you will own first.\\\\n8. Any open questions/coordination points with FE & BE.\\\\nReturn a deterministic plan only, no code yet.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DEVOPS_PLAN_v1\\\"\\n}\\n###orderDevOps1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:20:21.890Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"orderFE1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order FE_PLAN_v1 from agent 4: INITIAL PLANNING REPORT – HOA WEBSITE FRONTEND (Next.js)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Page / Route Tree (Next.js 13 app router)\\\\\\\\n• /                             → Home (public)\\\\\\\\n• /(public)/contact-us          → Contact Us (public)\\\\\\\\n• /(public)/bylaws              → Bylaws (public)\\\\\\\\n• /(public)/schools             → Schools (public)\\\\\\\\n• /(auth)/login                 → Login (public – redirect if already authed)\\\\\\\\n• /(auth)/signup                → Signup (public – redirect if already authed)\\\\\\\\n• /(protected)/* (group guarded by auth)\\\\\\\\n    • /dues                     → Pay / view dues\\\\\\\\n    • /annual-meetings          → Meeting minutes & docs\\\\\\\\n    • /news-events              → Neighborhood news & events\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSupporting file: middleware.ts will redirect unauthenticated users who hit /(protected) paths.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Component & Layout Strategy\\\\\\\\nRoot Layout (app/layout.tsx)\\\\\\\\n    ├── <Navbar/>  → dynamic links based on auth/role\\\\\\\\n    ├── <main/>    → page content via children\\\\\\\\n    └── <Footer/>\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nProtected Group Layout (app/(protected)/layout.tsx)\\\\\\\\n    └── <AuthGuard> (context hook + redirect) → children\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nKey Reusable Components\\\\\\\\n• Navbar.tsx        – top-level navigation, shows different menus for guest/user\\\\\\\\n• Footer.tsx        – site-wide footer\\\\\\\\n• AuthGuard.tsx     – redirects unauthenticated users (only used by protected layout)\\\\\\\\n• FormInput.tsx / Button.tsx (later) – small shared UI atoms (OFAAT when needed)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Responsive Design & Styling Approach\\\\\\\\nChoice: Tailwind CSS (utility-first) + @tailwindcss/typography.\\\\\\\\nRationale:  ✔ rapid prototyping, ✔ highly maintainable with small team, ✔ excellent purging, ✔ large ecosystem, ✔ minimal runtime cost. No extra runtime dependency like styled-components.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Firebase Authentication Flow (v9 modular SDK)\\\\\\\\n• lib/firebase.ts – initializes Firebase app & exports auth object.\\\\\\\\n• contexts/AuthContext.tsx – wraps onAuthStateChanged, stores {user, role, loading}.\\\\\\\\n• hooks/useAuth.ts – convenient consumer hook.\\\\\\\\n• Login/Page – email-password (and Google provider placeholder) → signInWithEmailAndPassword / signInWithPopup.\\\\\\\\n• Signup/Page – createUserWithEmailAndPassword.\\\\\\\\n• Session Persistence – setPersistence(browserLocalPersistence) on app init.\\\\\\\\n• Role Handling – user’s custom claim “role” fetched via getIdTokenResult; stored in context for conditional UI (e.g., board-member extras).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Additional Contexts / Hooks\\\\\\\\n• useAuthGuard.ts – returns boolean & can trigger router.push('/auth/login').\\\\\\\\n• (Optional) useToast.ts (later) – global feedback.\\\\\\\\nNo other global providers currently required.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Source Files / Modules (OFAAT LIST)\\\\\\\\n(Each bullet = one physical file with 1-sentence purpose.)\\\\\\\\n• app/layout.tsx                     – Root HTML skeleton + Navbar/Footer inclusion.\\\\\\\\n• app/page.tsx                       – Public Home page content.\\\\\\\\n• app/(public)/contact-us/page.tsx   – Contact information page.\\\\\\\\n• app/(public)/bylaws/page.tsx       – HOA bylaws page.\\\\\\\\n• app/(public)/schools/page.tsx      – Local schools info page.\\\\\\\\n• app/(auth)/layout.tsx              – Simple layout for auth pages (centered card).\\\\\\\\n• app/(auth)/login/page.tsx          – Login form & auth logic.\\\\\\\\n• app/(auth)/signup/page.tsx         – Signup form & auth logic.\\\\\\\\n• app/(protected)/layout.tsx         – Wraps children with <AuthGuard>.\\\\\\\\n• app/(protected)/dues/page.tsx      – Member dues dashboard.\\\\\\\\n• app/(protected)/annual-meetings/page.tsx – Annual meeting resources.\\\\\\\\n• app/(protected)/news-events/page.tsx      – News & events feed.\\\\\\\\n• components/Navbar.tsx              – Responsive top navigation bar.\\\\\\\\n• components/Footer.tsx              – Site footer.\\\\\\\\n• components/AuthGuard.tsx           – Redirect component for auth gating.\\\\\\\\n• contexts/AuthContext.tsx           – Provides user/session context.\\\\\\\\n• hooks/useAuth.ts                   – Hook to access AuthContext easily.\\\\\\\\n• hooks/useAuthGuard.ts              – Hook implementing auth/redirect logic.\\\\\\\\n• lib/firebase.ts                    – Firebase initialization & helpers.\\\\\\\\n• middleware.ts                      – Next.js edge middleware enforcing auth on /(protected).\\\\\\\\n• styles/globals.css                 – Tailwind base & custom CSS imports.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. External Dependencies (add to package.json)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"firebase\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^9.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tailwindcss\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^3.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"postcss\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^8.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"autoprefixer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^10.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"clsx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^1.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (utility for conditional classes),\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"@headlessui/react\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^1.x\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (accessible UI elements; optional but planned),\\\\\\\\n(Next.js, React, React-DOM already present via create-next-app.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Open Questions / Assumptions\\\\\\\\n• Roles – Only basic \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"member\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" vs \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"? Any admin dashboard needed later?\\\\\\\\n• Data Layer – Are Firestore reads/writes in scope for this phase or strictly auth guard + static content?\\\\\\\\n• Branding – Color palette / fonts provided or should Tailwind defaults with slight customization be used?\\\\\\\\n• Hosting – Will Firebase Hosting be used? (affects auth domain whitelisting & deployment scripts.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeterminism Note:  All filenames and route segments above are final unless new requirements emerge, ensuring downstream teams can reference them with confidence.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"orderBE1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order BE_PLAN_v1 from agent 3: INITIAL PLANNING REPORT – HOA BACKEND & FIREBASE INTEGRATION\\\\\\\\nPrepared by: Director Vader\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n0. Guiding Principles\\\\\\\\n   • Least-privilege, role-based, document-level security.  \\\\\\\\n   • OFAAT: each subsequent ticket will tackle one collection, one rule file, or one API route at a time.  \\\\\\\\n   • Avoid custom servers where possible; leverage Firebase Admin SDK inside Next.js API routes for privileged ops.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Firebase Project Layout\\\\\\\\n   a. Services Enabled\\\\\\\\n      – Authentication (Email/Password + Google OIDC provider)\\\\\\\\n      – Cloud Firestore (native mode)  \\\\\\\\n      – Cloud Storage (1 bucket, folder-based ACL)  \\\\\\\\n      – Cloud Functions (optional: re-validation, image/PDF processing)  \\\\\\\\n      – Firebase Hosting (only if we decide to deploy SSR there; otherwise Vercel hosts Next.js)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   b. Core Firestore Collections / Canonical Document Shapes\\\\\\\\n      • users/{uid}\\\\\\\\n        └─ displayName  : string\\\\\\\\n        └─ email        : string\\\\\\\\n        └─ roles        : array<string>   // [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resident\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"], [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"], [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"admin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\n        └─ unitNumber   : string          // optional HOA-specific meta\\\\\\\\n        └─ createdAt / updatedAt  (serverTimestamp)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • roles/{roleId}  (static docs, rarely change)\\\\\\\\n        └─ permissions : map<string, bool> // e.g. {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"canUploadMinutes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": true}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • pages/{slug}\\\\\\\\n        └─ title        : string\\\\\\\\n        └─ contentRich  : string (MDX)\\\\\\\\n        └─ visibility   : \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"public\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resident\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n        └─ updatedBy    : uid\\\\\\\\n        └─ updatedAt\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • posts/{postId}\\\\\\\\n        └─ title, body, publishedAt, visibility (same enum)\\\\\\\\n        └─ tags: array<string>\\\\\\\\n        └─ authorUid\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • minutes/{year}/{docId}\\\\\\\\n        └─ meetingDate  : Timestamp\\\\\\\\n        └─ storagePath  : string // gs:// bucket path to PDF\\\\\\\\n        └─ uploadedBy   : uid\\\\\\\\n        └─ visibility   : \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resident\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n        └─ createdAt\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • financialReports/{fiscalYear}/{docId}\\\\\\\\n        (same shape as minutes)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n      • siteSettings/singleton\\\\\\\\n        └─ maintenanceMode : bool, etc.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Security-Rules Outline (high-level)\\\\\\\\n   • Meta: One ruleset file; use custom auth token claims (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"roles\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": [..]) to discriminate.  \\\\\\\\n   • Rule skeleton per collection:\\\\\\\\n     match /users/{uid}           { allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid; allow write: if request.auth.uid == uid; }\\\\\\\\n     match /roles/{roleId}        { allow read: if request.auth.token.roles.hasAny([\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"admin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]); }\\\\\\\\n     match /pages/{slug}          { allow read: if doc.visibility==\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"public\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || (roleResident&&doc.visibility==\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resident\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") || (roleBoard && doc.visibility==\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"); allow write: if roleBoard; }\\\\\\\\n     match /posts/{id}            { similar to pages; write restricted to board/admin }\\\\\\\\n     match /minutes/{year}/{id}   { allow read: if roleResident || roleBoard; write: if roleBoard; }\\\\\\\\n     match /financialReports/{y}/{id} { read: roleResident||roleBoard; write: roleBoard; }\\\\\\\\n   • Storage rules mirror Firestore: gs://<bucket>/minutes/** etc.  \\\\\\\\n   • Custom claims population handled by Cloud Function triggered on onCreate user or manual admin API.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Storage Bucket & Paths\\\\\\\\n   • Single bucket: gs://hoa-site-assets\\\\\\\\n   • Folder prefixes:\\\\\\\\n       /minutes/<yyyy>/<filename.pdf>\\\\\\\\n       /financial/<fiscalYear>/<filename.pdf>\\\\\\\\n       /postImages/<postId>/<img.png>\\\\\\\\n   • Firebase Storage security rules use path-based wildcards to map to user roles (same claims as Firestore).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Admin-Level CMS Workflows\\\\\\\\n   • Admin/Board users access an /admin dashboard (Next.js page).  \\\\\\\\n   • All privileged mutations use Next.js API routes that initialize Admin SDK with service-account key (server-only).  \\\\\\\\n   • Upload files via signed upload URLs (generated in API route) or via the client SDK gated by role claims.  \\\\\\\\n   • Versioning: keep previous versions by writing new docId; optionally mark latestVersion flag.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Next.js Server-Side Helpers & API Endpoints (OFAAT list)\\\\\\\\n   1. POST /api/auth/setCustomClaims          → sets roles array for given uid (Admin only)\\\\\\\\n   2. GET  /api/pages/[slug]                  → SSR-friendly retrieval (public or gated)\\\\\\\\n   3. GET  /api/posts?visibility=x            → list posts for feed\\\\\\\\n   4. POST /api/admin/posts                   → create/update post (board)\\\\\\\\n   5. POST /api/admin/minutes/uploadUrl       → returns signed URL + Firestore stub\\\\\\\\n   6. GET  /api/minutes/[year]                → list minutes metadata user can view\\\\\\\\n   7. GET  /api/financial/[fiscalYear]        → list financial reports\\\\\\\\n   8. POST /api/admin/financial/uploadUrl     → same pattern\\\\\\\\n   9. GET  /api/user/profile                  → returns user doc for self-service editing\\\\\\\\n  10. PATCH /api/user/profile                 → update profile fields\\\\\\\\n   (Each endpoint is one future task.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Environment Variables / Secrets (hand-off to DevOps)\\\\\\\\n   • FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID\\\\\\\\n   • FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL\\\\\\\\n   • FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY        (service-account for Admin SDK)\\\\\\\\n   • NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY\\\\\\\\n   • NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN\\\\\\\\n   • NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET\\\\\\\\n   • NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID\\\\\\\\n   • NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID\\\\\\\\n   • (Optional) GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET for OIDC\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Auth Flow Text Diagram\\\\\\\\n   [Client] → signInWithEmail/Google via Firebase JS SDK\\\\\\\\n        ↳ Firebase Auth issues ID Token (incl. custom role claims)\\\\\\\\n   [Client] → passes ID Token as bearer to Next.js API\\\\\\\\n   [API Route] → verifyIdToken via firebase-admin; obtains uid + roles\\\\\\\\n        ↳ Authorize logic; perform Firestore/Storage ops with elevated service account if needed\\\\\\\\n   [API Route] → responds JSON → [Client]\\\\\\\\n   Next.js pages with getServerSideProps can also verify ID token via cookies for SSR.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Open Questions / Risks\\\\\\\\n   • Do we need granular committee-level roles beyond resident/board/admin?\\\\\\\\n   • File sizes for minutes/financials—will Storage free tier suffice?  \\\\\\\\n   • GDPR/PII: any resident data visibility concerns (e.g., addresses)?\\\\\\\\n   • Offline access? (Service Worker caching PDFs?)\\\\\\\\n   • Disaster recovery / backup strategy for Firestore & Storage.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Deterministic Execution Plan (high-level)\\\\\\\\n   Step P1  – Finalize data model JSON schematic (OFAAT per collection).\\\\\\\\n   Step P2  – Draft Firestore & Storage security rules skeleton; review with Partagaz D.\\\\\\\\n   Step P3  – Implement auth helper in Next.js (Admin SDK singleton)\\\\\\\\n   Step P4  – Build /api/pages/[slug] endpoint as template (patterns for gating)\\\\\\\\n   Step P5  – Implement admin post create endpoint & page upload flow\\\\\\\\n   Step P6  – Iterate through remaining endpoints (minutes, financials, profile)\\\\\\\\n   Step P7  – QA + unit tests covering role-based access\\\\\\\\n   Step P8  – Hand off .env.example & secret injection instructions to DevOps\\\\\\\\n   Step P9  – Documentation & knowledge transfer\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nReport complete.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"orderDevOps1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order DEVOPS_PLAN_v1 from agent 2: **INITIAL PLANNING REPORT – REPOSITORY / DEPLOYMENT / NON-TECHNICAL CMS WORKFLOW**  \\\\\\\\nAuthor: Director – DevOps & CMS Experience  \\\\\\\\nDate: {{timestamp}}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1. Repository Scaffolding & Structure\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1.1 Monorepo vs Poly-repo Rationale  \\\\\\\\n• Monorepo (single Git repo) favored – scope is a single Next.js app + infra scripts.  \\\\\\\\n• Pros: unified versioning, simpler CI, single source of truth for env schema, easier atomic PR reviews.  \\\\\\\\n• Cons (build times) mitigated via Vercel’s per-path caching.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.2 Proposed Layout\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n/<repo-root>\\\\\\\\n ├─ apps/\\\\\\\\n │   └─ web/                 # Next.js 14 (app-router) application\\\\\\\\n ├─ infra/                   # Terraform or CLIs for Firebase & Vercel linking (later)\\\\\\\\n ├─ scripts/                 # one-off maintenance + CMS backup scripts\\\\\\\\n ├─ .github/\\\\\\\\n │   └─ workflows/           # lint / type-check gate (optional)\\\\\\\\n ├─ .env.schema              # commented sample of required env keys\\\\\\\\n ├─ .editorconfig\\\\\\\\n ├─ package.json / pnpm-workspace.yaml (pnpm workspaces) \\\\\\\\n └─ README.md\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.3 Environment-File Strategy  \\\\\\\\n• Local: `.env.local`, ignored by VCS.  \\\\\\\\n• Shared (non-secret): `.env.defaults` committed for DX.  \\\\\\\\n• CI: Vercel env manager (broken into `Development`, `Preview`, `Production` scopes).  \\\\\\\\n• Validation: `env.ts` with `zod` schema – build fails if key missing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n2. CI / CD & Access Control\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n2.1 Vercel Integration  \\\\\\\\n• GitHub App connected – push to any branch triggers “Preview Deployment”.  \\\\\\\\n• `main` branch auto-promotes to Production after PR review.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2.2 Protected Env Vars  \\\\\\\\n• Secrets entered only in Vercel UI → Production scope accessible to “Developers” team only.  \\\\\\\\n• Board/Content Editors assigned “Viewer” role – can trigger CMS edits but cannot read env vars.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2.3 Role Matrix\\\\\\\\n| Role | GitHub | Vercel | Firebase |\\\\\\\\n|------|--------|--------|----------|\\\\\\\\n| Dev  | Write  | Developer | Editor |\\\\\\\\n| Board | Triage | Viewer   | Custom `contentEditor` IAM |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n3. Bootstrap Commands / Tooling\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n# 1. Create project\\\\\\\\npnpm create next-app web --ts --eslint --tailwind --app\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 2. Workspace root tooling\\\\\\\\npnpm add -w -D prettier eslint-config-next husky lint-staged commitlint @commitlint/config-conventional\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 3. Init Husky hooks\\\\\\\\nnpx husky install && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pnpm lint\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | npx husky add .husky/pre-commit\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 4. Tailwind already scaffolded; add prettier-plugin-tailwindcss\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\nAll commands will be scripted in `scripts/bootstrap.sh` for deterministic provisioning.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n4. Admin / CMS Approach Choice\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nOptions considered:\\\\\\\\na) Custom / Firestore + TipTap  \\\\\\\\n   • Fine-grained control, reuse Firebase stack.  \\\\\\\\n   • Requires bespoke rich-text, auth, image management.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nb) Headless Markdown CMS (TinaCMS in-repo)  \\\\\\\\n   • Zero external backend; content = Markdown files → versioned in Git.  \\\\\\\\n   • Visual editing UI runs inside Next.js; non-technical editors get WYSIWYG; PR flow optional.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDecision: **TinaCMS (b)**  \\\\\\\\nRationale: board members prefer low-friction, WYSIWYG; having content as Markdown keeps backup & diff via Git; avoids building a separate secured admin panel; fastest to ship.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n5. Provisioning Steps (Vercel & Firebase)\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1. Create Firebase project `org-site`  \\\\\\\\n2. Enable Authentication (GitHub SSO) & Firestore (if we need additional dynamic data)  \\\\\\\\n3. Generate service-account JSON → store in 1Password, inject into Vercel as `FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT` (Base64).  \\\\\\\\n4. In Vercel:  \\\\\\\\n   • Import GitHub repo → define `NEXT_PUBLIC_` keys, `FIREBASE_` secrets.  \\\\\\\\n   • Map Production branch `main`.  \\\\\\\\n5. Install Tina Cloud → obtain `TINA_PUBLIC_CLIENT_ID` & `TINA_TOKEN` → add to Vercel.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n6. Branching, Tagging & Content Backup\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• `main` – protected; deploys Production.  \\\\\\\\n• `feat/*`, `fix/*` – short-lived.  \\\\\\\\n• Release tags `vX.Y.Z` created on merge to `main`.  \\\\\\\\n• Content backup: Markdown lives in repo → automatic via Git; additionally Vercel’s “git-backup” retained. Optional nightly action mirroring `content/` to S3 bucket for redundancy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n7. File(s) I Will Own First (OFAAT)\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n`infra/vercel-firebase-setup.md` – step-by-step deterministic instructions including CLI commands & env schemas. (Subsequent infra scripts after approval.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n8. Open Questions / Coordination Points\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1. FE Lead: confirm desire for Tina’s sidebar editing UI vs dedicated `/admin` route.  \\\\\\\\n2. BE Lead: need Firestore at all beyond Tina? (e.g., dynamic surveys) – impacts IAM & costs.  \\\\\\\\n3. Design: tailwind base palette / typography token decisions required before enforcing lint-staged style rules.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nEND OF REPORT\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-28T00:20:21.890Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ask-user-1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"Resolve architectural decisions (content management method, role hierarchy) and gather design inputs before Directors proceed to code.\\\",\\n  \\